2. Explain TLS, PKI, certificate, public key, private key and signature.
TLS: a protocol for secure communication between client and server. When we hit a new website, tls establishes
several handshakes to verify the server's identity. First client side say hello, sending info like encryption
algorithms and tls version it supports. Then server side say hello, sending certificate to browser. After validating
this server's identify, key exchange happens. Such secret key will be used in subsequent communication to secure
data. Now client and server can communicate in a safe environment.
PKI: public key infrastructure. It includes, public key, private key, certificate authority, certificate, it is
sent from server to browser so the browser can validate its identity.
certificate: for identification, includes public key, issuer info, server info, expiration date.
public key: used in asymmetric encryption, to encrypt data.
private key: used in asymmetric encryption, to decrypt data.
signature: digital signature to prove the info is not changed

